iv><div className="landscape-pillars"><div><b>01</b><strong>PER-DEVICE<br />DIGITAL TWINS</strong><p>Each device learns its own expected behavior.</p></div><div><b>02</b><strong>HYBRID<br />DETECTION</strong><p>ML, distribution drift, and deterministic rules.</p></div><div><b>03</b><strong>OPERATIONAL<br />TRUST</strong><p>Detection signals become an actionable state.</p></div><div><b>04</b><strong>VERIFIED<br />RECOVERY</strong><p>Trust returns only after clean behavior is observed.</p></div></div><div className="lifecycle-explorer"><div className="landscape-heading"><div><span className="eyebrow">From detection to recovery</span><h3>Follow the Aegis response loop</h3></div></div><div className="lifecycle-tabs">{lifecycleStages.map((item) => <button className={item.id === lifecycleId ? "active" : ""} key={item.id} onClick={() => setLifecycleId(item.id)}>{item.label}</button>)}</div><div className="lifecycle-detail"><div><strong>{lifecycle.title}</strong><p>{lifecycle.text}</p></div><span>{lifecycle.evidence}</span></div></div><div className="comparison-table-wrap"><span className="eyebrow">Capability comparison</span><table className="comparison-table"><thead><tr><th>Capability</th><th>Enterprise resilience</th><th>Kyndryl framework</th><th>Aegis-Twin</th></tr></thead><tbody>{comparisonRows.map((row) => <tr key={row[0]}>{row.map((cell) => <td key={cell}>{cell}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody></table></div><div className="landscape-closing"><span>OTHERS ASK: <b>WAS THE ATTACK DETECTED?</b></span><strong>AEGIS ASKS: DID THE DEVICE RECOVER, AND CAN WE PROVE IT?</strong><p>Aegis-Twin turns detection into measurable, device-level resilience.</p></div></section>;
